        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does Schwinn Still Make Treadmills? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes. Schwinn Fitness sold four treadmills in the US on 5 November 2026: the SFT4.1 at $699, the
            810 and SFT4.3 at $899 each, and the 815 at $1,099. For most buyers the SFT4.3 is the one to pick, because
            it gives a 60-inch deck and 12 mph for the 810's price.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            All four fold, all four plug into a standard household outlet, and all four carry the same warranty: 10
            years on the frame and drive motor and one year on mechanical parts, electronics and labour. None of
            Schwinn's product pages gives a motor rating. They are best seen as walking and light jogging machines,
            with the 60-inch 815 and SFT4.3 the only ones long enough for regular running.
          </p>
          <p>
            The 830, which still draws searches, is no longer on Schwinn's US store, and nor is the 870. If you are
            choosing between a Schwinn and anything else, Bowflex's T6 — from the same owner — was $999 on sale on the
            day we checked, and offered a steeper incline and a lifetime motor warranty for $100 more than the 815. The T6
            is sold by Bowflex rather than on Amazon, so the step-up card below shows the Horizon 7.0 AT, another Johnson
            Health Tech machine with 15% incline that Amazon does sell. We
            have not tested any of these treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-owns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Makes Schwinn Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Schwinn Fitness is a separate business from Schwinn bicycles. The bicycles belong to Pacific Cycle; the
            fitness equipment was built up for two decades by Nautilus Inc., which also owned Bowflex and sold Nautilus
            treadmills. Nautilus Inc. renamed itself BowFlex Inc. in 2023 and filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024, and
            Johnson Health Tech bought its assets that April. Coverage of the sale lists the BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY
            brands, and Johnson Health Tech said it would fold the BowFlex and Schwinn operations into its North
            American retail and e-commerce business.
          </p>
          <p>
            Schwinn Fitness's site does not name its parent, but its careers link points to Johnson Health Tech. That
            puts Schwinn treadmills alongside Horizon, Matrix, Vision and Bowflex in one group. Commercial Schwinn indoor-cycling
            bikes have long been made under licence by a different company, Core Health &amp; Fitness, which is why
            gym Schwinn equipment and home Schwinn treadmills come from different places.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why it matters to a buyer</h3>
          <p>
            Two things follow. First, warranty claims and parts for current Schwinn treadmills go through Schwinn Fitness,
            now part of Johnson Health Tech, rather than a company that no longer exists. Second, there is now some overlap between Schwinn and Bowflex: the
            $1,099 815 and the $999-on-sale Bowflex T6 compete for the same buyer, and the <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand guide</a> sets out the rest of that
            range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Current Schwinn Treadmill Range</h2>
<p>
            Schwinn's four treadmills fall into two pairs: the 55-inch, 10 mph SFT4.1 and 810, and the 60-inch, 12 mph
            SFT4.3 and 815. The table uses Schwinn's own figures from its product pages on 5 November 2026; the cards
            below show where independent sources agree.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed / incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Max user (Schwinn)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Weight</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SFT4.1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph / 10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">180 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">810</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$899</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph / 10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">275 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">155 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SFT4.3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$899</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph / 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">187 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">815</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,099</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph / 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">187 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Schwinn 810</h3>
          <p>
            The 810 is the Schwinn most people know, and the one with real independent coverage. Garage Gym Reviews,
            BarBend and Best Buy's listing agree with Schwinn on a 20 by 55-inch belt, 10 mph, 10% motorized incline,
            a 275 lb user limit and a SoftDrop fold. Reviewers describe it as a <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>budget treadmill</a> for walking and light
            running and note that its 55-inch belt is short for taller runners. The motor is where sources part:
            Garage Gym Reviews gives 2.6 continuous horsepower, Best Buy's listing 1.5 HP, and Schwinn's own page gives
            none. That spread is too wide to resolve, so neither figure is on the card. One review we saw quoted a
            $2,499 MSRP, which matches no listing we found; Schwinn, Fitness Supply and other retailers had it at $899.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The SFT4.3 and 815</h3>
          <p>
            These two share a frame: Schwinn lists both at 76 by 34 by 57 inches, 48 inches long folded, 187 lb and
            325 lb maximum user. Both have a 20 by 60-inch running area, 12 mph and 12% incline, EasyDial controls and
            a 5-inch LCD. The 815 adds eight programs (against six), Bluetooth heart-rate support, USB charging and
            listed app integrations with JRNY, Peloton and <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a>. That is $200 of extras on the same deck. One retailer
            listing gave the SFT4.3's capacity as 275 lb, conflicting with Schwinn's 325 lb, which is why we have left
            capacity off its card.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The SFT4.1</h3>
          <p>
            The SFT4.1 is the plainest Schwinn and the cheapest: three LED windows, five programs, a fan, speakers and a
            tablet holder on the same 20 by 55-inch, 10 mph, 10% layout as the 810, but with a 300 lb user rating and a
            $200 lower price. For a walker, it undercuts the 810 on almost every count except console and weight.
          </p>
        </div>
