        <div id="older-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Older Schwinn Treadmills: the 830 and 870</h2>
<p>
            Two older Schwinn models still draw searches and appear second-hand. Neither was on Schwinn's US store on 5
            November 2026, and an old Schwinn 830 page that surfaced in search results carried a notice that new orders
            were paused.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Schwinn 830</h3>
          <p>
            TreadmillReviews.com and Garage Gym Reviews agree on a 2.75 continuous horsepower motor, a 20 by 55-inch
            running area, 12 mph, 0 to 12% incline, a 300 lb user rating, 22 programs and a hydraulic fold.
            TreadmillReviews.com lists a 10-year frame, two-year mechanical and one-year electrical and labour warranty,
            and a $949 price when new. Some searches attribute a 22 by 60-inch deck to it; we found nothing to support
            that, and the 60-inch belt belongs to the 870. The 830 was the predecessor of today's 810 and 815, built
            under Nautilus Inc.; it was never recalled.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Schwinn 870</h3>
          <p>
            The 870 was the top Schwinn treadmill of that generation, with a 20 by 60-inch belt, 0 to 15% incline and a
            3.0 CHP motor in independent reviews, and is listed as discontinued. Treat a used 870 or 830 as you would
            any treadmill of that age: no transferable warranty, parts from Johnson Health Tech or third-party
            suppliers, and a careful inspection. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers what to look at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motors-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Two Things Schwinn Does Not Tell You Enough About</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor ratings</h3>
          <p>
            None of Schwinn's four product pages gives the motor's horsepower, continuous or peak. For the 810, outside
            sources range from 1.5 HP to 2.6 CHP; for the 815, one deal listing said 3 HP. Without a manufacturer
            figure, the best guides to what each machine is built for are the <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt length</a>, top speed and user limit,
            and those point to walking and jogging, not daily running. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains why continuous ratings matter more than peak figures, if you find one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Every current Schwinn treadmill carries 10 years on the frame and drive motor and one year on mechanical
            parts, electronics and labour. The 10-year motor term is good for the price, but the one-year electronics
            term is short: console boards and motor controllers are among the parts that fail on budget treadmills, and
            after year one they are on you. Compare that with Bowflex's lifetime motor cover, or Horizon's longer parts
            terms, before deciding. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> sets out which terms matter most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Schwinn Treadmill Recalls</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Schwinn, Nautilus and Johnson
            Health Tech on 5 November 2026. No recall names a Schwinn treadmill. The recent Schwinn-titled recalls are
            Pacific Cycle's — electric scooters in 2021 and bicycle child carriers in 2025 — and concern the bicycle
            company, not Schwinn Fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            The closest treadmill recall in the family is the 2022 recall of about 7,300 Nautilus T616 and T618
            treadmills sold in 2021, which can start on their own. Nautilus Inc. made those under its other brand,
            and the CPSC now says no remedy is available. If you are shopping second-hand and a seller offers a
            "Nautilus/Schwinn" treadmill, read our <a href="/nautilus-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Nautilus treadmills guide</a> to check the serial number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Schwinn Treadmill Should You Buy?</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>For walking on a budget:</strong> the SFT4.1 at $699. It matches the 810's belt, speed and
            incline, with a higher user rating, for $200 less.</li>
            <li><strong>For walking and some jogging:</strong> the SFT4.3 at $899. The 60-inch deck and 12 mph make it
            the most versatile Schwinn for the money.</li>
            <li><strong>For apps:</strong> the 815 at $1,099, if JRNY, Peloton or Zwift integration matters to you.</li>
            <li><strong>For the 810:</strong> buy it if you want the lightest machine (155 lb) or the one with the most
            independent coverage, and you are under 275 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>For running or more incline:</strong> step up to a Bowflex T6 or a <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT</a>, both
            Johnson Health Tech machines with 15% incline.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Heavier users should look past the Schwinn range altogether; our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a> covers machines rated well above 325 lb. For more options in the same price band, see our <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $1,000</a> and <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where to Buy a Schwinn Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Schwinn Fitness sells all four models direct, and on 5 November 2026 each showed as in stock on its US
            store. The 810 is also widely stocked by retailers: we saw it at Best Buy and Fitness Supply, at the same
            $899, and Schwinn treadmills appear at times in <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Best Buy's treadmill range</a> and at <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Dick's Sporting Goods</a>.
            Retail listings for the newer SFT models were patchier, and some showed different figures from Schwinn's own
            pages.
          </p>
          <p>
            On Amazon, the Schwinn 810 has a listing we have matched to the "Schwinn Fitness 810 Treadmill", so its card
            button goes straight to it. The other Schwinn cards use tagged searches, because we could not tie an Amazon
            listing to each exact model. Check that the seller is Schwinn, Amazon or an authorised retailer, which keeps
            the warranty registration simple; our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a> explains how.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Schwinn Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Schwinn treadmills are good value if you buy the right one for the job. These are the mistakes we see most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the 810 to run on</h3>
              <p>Its 55-inch belt and 10 mph top speed suit walking and light jogging. If you plan to run, the SFT4.3 costs the same and gives a 60-inch deck and 12 mph.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the user limit</h3>
              <p>The 810 is rated to 275 lb, the lowest in the range, and one retailer gives the SFT4.3 the same figure against Schwinn’s 325 lb. Leave a margin below the limit, especially for running.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the 10-year warranty covers everything</h3>
              <p>It covers the frame and drive motor. Parts, electronics and labour are covered for one year. A failed console board in year two is a paid repair.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying $1,099 for features you will not use</h3>
              <p>The 815 and SFT4.3 share a deck, frame and speed. The $200 difference buys app integrations and two more programs; if you will not use JRNY, Peloton or Zwift, buy the SFT4.3.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Schwinn still sells four folding treadmills in the US, from $699 to $1,099, made
          now by Johnson Health Tech.</strong> The SFT4.3 is the best value, with a 60-inch deck and 12 mph for $899;
          the SFT4.1 is the budget walker; the well-known 810 suits one user under 275 lb who mainly walks. Schwinn
          publishes no motor ratings, and parts and electronics are covered for only a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want more incline and a longer motor warranty, the <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Bowflex T6</a> from the same owner was $999 on sale on the day we checked.
          </p>
        </div>

