        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Does Schwinn still sell treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. On 5 November 2026 Schwinn Fitness’s US store sold four treadmills: the SFT4.1 at $699, the 810 and SFT4.3 at $899 each and the 815 at $1,099, all showing in stock. The older 830 and 870 are no longer sold.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who makes Schwinn treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Johnson Health Tech, the Taiwanese group behind Horizon, Matrix and Vision. It bought the assets of BowFlex Inc., formerly Nautilus Inc., including the Schwinn Fitness brand, in April 2024. Schwinn bicycles belong to a different company, Pacific Cycle.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the Schwinn 810 treadmill specs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Schwinn lists a 20 by 55-inch belt, 0 to 10 mph, 0 to 10% motorized incline, a 275 lb maximum user, 155 lb weight and a SoftDrop fold, with 16 programs; independent reviews agree. Schwinn gives no motor rating, and outside figures conflict, from 1.5 HP to 2.6 CHP.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Schwinn 830 discontinued?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is no longer on Schwinn’s US store. Reviews give it a 2.75 CHP motor, a 20 by 55-inch belt, 12 mph, 12% incline and a 300 lb user rating, at $949 when new. The current 815 and SFT4.3 replace it with a longer 60-inch deck.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty do Schwinn treadmills have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              All four current models carry 10 years on the frame and drive motor and one year on mechanical parts, electronics and labour, according to Schwinn’s product pages on 5 November 2026. The one-year electronics term is short compared with Bowflex’s and Horizon’s.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Schwinn 810 or SFT4.3 better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most buyers, the SFT4.3. Both cost $899 at Schwinn, but the SFT4.3 has a 60-inch rather than 55-inch deck, 12 mph rather than 10 and 12% rather than 10% incline. The 810 is lighter and better reviewed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Have any Schwinn treadmills been recalled?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We found none in the CPSC database on 5 November 2026. Recent Schwinn-titled recalls concern Pacific Cycle’s bicycle products. The related Nautilus T616 and T618 treadmills, made by the company that then owned Schwinn Fitness, were recalled in 2022.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
